/* WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: TO STOP CLOUDFLARE GUESSING.
 *
 * This site is `output: 'static'` — 63 prerendered HTML pages and no server.
 * There is nothing to run at a request. So the deploy is an upload, and this
 * file says exactly that: serve ./dist, and nothing else.
 *
 * THE FAILURE IT FIXES. Without a wrangler config, `wrangler deploy` runs
 * AUTOCONFIG: it detects the framework and, for Astro, installs
 * `@astrojs/cloudflare` and rewrites the build to on-demand rendering. Astro
 * then boots a miniflare worker to run getStaticPaths for /bags/[slug], that
 * worker 500s, and the build dies:
 *
 *     [build] Rearranging server assets...
 *      generating static routes
 *     Failed to get static paths from the Cloudflare prerender server (500)
 *     TypeError: Invalid URL string.
 *       at async Object.fetch (node_modules/miniflare/.../entry.worker.js)
 *
 * A build that succeeds locally and on a clean clone, failing only on the host,
 * because the host quietly changed the rendering mode.
 *
 * I FIRST BLAMED THE WRONG THING. `@astrojs/cloudflare` was sitting unused in
 * package.json, so I removed it and expected that to be the end of it. The next
 * build printed the identical error — autoconfig had reinstalled it. Removing
 * the dependency was right for tidiness and irrelevant to the bug: the trigger
 * is the ABSENCE OF THIS FILE, not the presence of that package.
 *
 * So the load-bearing line here is the one that is missing. There is no `main`.
 * A Worker with assets and no entrypoint cannot execute anything, which is a
 * true description of this site and the reason autoconfig has nothing left to
 * infer.
 *
 * Astro's own docs carry this exact shape under "Static":
 * https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/deploy/cloudflare/
 */
{
  /* Must match the Worker in the dashboard — sizer.phranque-y.workers.dev. */
  "name": "sizer",

  /* Pin the runtime the day we deploy, so a future Cloudflare release cannot
     change behaviour under a site nobody is watching. Bump it deliberately. */
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",

  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist"

    /* NO not_found_handling YET, and that is a real gap rather than a default
       we are happy with. Astro only emits dist/404.html if src/pages/404.astro
       exists, and it does not, so pointing at a 404 page would point at
       nothing. Add the page first, then add:
           "not_found_handling": "404-page"
       Until then a bad URL gets Cloudflare's own 404, which is unbranded but
       honest. */
  }
}
